WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.940
The change was reviewed and approved.

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.260
You'll find that sentence in a lot of outage reports.

00:00:06.867 --> 00:00:08.710
In June 2025, at Google Cloud,

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:12.887
a policy update with blank fields crashed Service Control worldwide.

00:00:13.687 --> 00:00:15.009
At Microsoft that October,

00:00:15.009 --> 00:00:18.112
valid customer configuration changes exposed a latent defect,

00:00:18.112 --> 00:00:20.707
and Azure Front Door's edge nodes started crashing.

00:00:21.607 --> 00:00:23.127
Both started as small changes.

00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:26.656
Changes are also arriving faster now,

00:00:26.656 --> 00:00:28.893
because an AI can draft one in seconds.

00:00:29.593 --> 00:00:30.675
Your reviewers are good,

00:00:30.675 --> 00:00:32.253
but what they're reading is a diff.

00:00:33.003 --> 00:00:34.486
A diff shows you what was typed.

00:00:34.486 --> 00:00:36.663
It doesn't show you what the network does next.

00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:39.807
Hammerhead rehearses the change first.

00:00:40.507 --> 00:00:42.904
It reads the configurations you already have,

00:00:42.904 --> 00:00:44.982
builds a working model of your network,

00:00:44.982 --> 00:00:46.367
and runs the change there.

00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:48.844
There are no agents to install,

00:00:48.844 --> 00:00:50.507
and no login on your devices.

00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:54.273
Here's a three-line routing edit that looks harmless.

00:00:55.073 --> 00:00:56.996
In the model, fifteen routes change,

00:00:56.996 --> 00:00:58.758
one device drops off the network,

00:00:58.758 --> 00:00:59.933
and the gate fails it.

00:01:00.883 --> 00:01:03.149
The cause wasn't in the lines anyone reviewed.

00:01:03.149 --> 00:01:04.921
It was at the other end of the link,

00:01:04.921 --> 00:01:06.103
in a file nobody opened.

00:01:07.003 --> 00:01:08.630
Your team finds that out at a desk,

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:10.303
instead of during the change window.

00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:14.633
Nothing about how you run changes has to move.

00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:16.883
Your engineers still approve,

00:01:16.883 --> 00:01:18.593
and your own tools still deploy.

00:01:19.243 --> 00:01:22.372
It doesn't matter whether a person or an AI wrote the change.

00:01:22.372 --> 00:01:23.911
It goes through the same gate,

00:01:23.911 --> 00:01:25.603
and if it fails, it doesn't ship.

00:01:26.253 --> 00:01:26.781
Afterwards,

00:01:26.781 --> 00:01:28.653
Hammerhead checks what actually landed.

00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:32.787
And when Hammerhead can't be sure, it says so.

00:01:33.637 --> 00:01:36.215
It has four answers. Pass, fail, advisory,

00:01:36.215 --> 00:01:36.952
and unknown.

00:01:36.952 --> 00:01:40.758
Unknown means part of the change is outside what it can model,

00:01:40.758 --> 00:01:42.477
and it tells you which part.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.360
So before your next change window,

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.420
run one real change through it.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.477
Pick the one somebody's a little nervous about.

00:01:51.477 --> 00:01:52.480
Break it here first.
